and every a mirror that shows another room led back to the same date, the same room, the same name. the Widow in White wasn't haunting the house. the Widow in White was the house, wearing it like a coat.\n\nhis best friend Sam brought salt, iron, sage — the whole apotropaic grocery list. Tom Riley brought a camera, a recorder, and the stubbornness that had gotten them this far. 'We document,' Tom Riley said. 'Then we end it.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Night', 'text': "his best friend Sam arrived — of course his best friend Sam did, called by the thing friends are — with headlights and a crowbar and no plan at all. Together they forced the Widow in White back toward the nursery, toward a mirror that shows another room, toward the broken place.\n\nThe scream, when it came, was relief. The cold spot warmed. The whispers stopped mid-word. Tom Riley and his best friend Sam stood in the sudden ordinary silence of the town of Gray's End, holding a mirror that shows another room, whole at last, and wept like children.\n\nTom Riley understood then what had to be done: not destroy a mirror that shows another room, but complete it. Join the halves. Speak the name. Give the Widow in White what it had wanted all along — to be seen, to be mourned, to be released."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Aftermath', 'text': "a mirror that shows another room, mended, sits on the mantel. It doesn't play, or fill, or show. It's just an object now — which is to say, it's just a story. Tom Riley keeps it to remember: some doors stay shut.\n\nMadame Zara sent a note: 'You did what I couldn't. The house is clean.' Tom Riley framed it. Not from pride — from proof. Proof that the dark can be answered.\n\nThe archives got a new entry, in Tom Riley's handwriting: what happened, what worked, what it cost. Let the next tenant know. Let no one ever pretend again."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue: The Light Kept', 'text': "some doors stay shut — Tom Riley wrote it on the first page of the archive, and on the last. Whoever comes next will read it. Whoever comes next will know.\n\nhis best friend Sam visits every anniversary with pancakes and bad jokes. They don't talk about that night. They don't need to. It's in the walls — but the walls, now, are just walls.\n\nAnd the town of Gray's End stands in its fog, ordinary and beloved, a house like any other — except that once, it was haunted, and once, someone stayed. That makes all the difference."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

