ide: dust, a small bed, and a music box that plays itself's twin — the other half of whatever had been broken that terrible night.\n\nThe whispers became a voice. It knew Ben Marsh's name. It offered a deal: leave the abandoned mill, leave a music box that plays itself, and walk away clean. Ben Marsh considered it for exactly as long as it took to be afraid — then refused."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Night', 'text': 'It came on the anniversary, as Ben Marsh knew it would. The fog pressed against the windows of the abandoned mill like a face. a music box that plays itself activated — played, filled, showed, rang, drew, unlocked — and the Widow in White stepped out of the walls.\n\nThe scream, when it came, was relief. The cold spot warmed. The whispers stopped mid-word. Ben Marsh and her colleague Priya stood in the sudden ordinary silence of the abandoned mill, holding a music box that plays itself, whole at last, and wept like children.\n\nBen Marsh understood then what had to be done: not destroy a music box that plays itself, but complete it. Join the halves. Speak the name. Give the Widow in White what it had wanted all along — to be seen, to be mourned, to be released.'}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Aftermath', 'text': "Dawn in the abandoned mill was just dawn — fog burning off, birds being birds. Ben Marsh slept fourteen hours and woke to her colleague Priya making pancakes like nothing had happened, which was exactly the right thing.\n\nthe abandoned mill is for sale again, and Ben Marsh is staying. Someone has to be here who knows. Someone has to keep the light on. That someone is Ben Marsh now.\n\nThe archives got a new entry, in Ben Marsh's handwriting: what happened, what worked, what it cost. Let the next tenant know. Let no one ever pretend again."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue: The Light Kept', 'text': "Years later, Ben Marsh still walks the abandoned mill at night, checking the rooms the way you check on sleeping children. The nursery is a study now. The cold spot is just a draft. Mostly.\n\nher colleague Priya visits every anniversary with pancakes and bad jokes. They don't talk about that night. They don't need to. It's in the walls — but the walls, now, are just walls.\n\nthe past never stays buried — Ben Marsh wrote it on the first page of the archive, and on the last. Whoever comes next will read it. Whoever comes next will know."}]}

